<Project
    title={frontmatter.title}
    subtitle=""
    description=`<p>
            Renaissance architecture reflects an idealized vision of the human
            body—proportioned, perfected, and measured against classical notions of
            harmony. Yet the human body is never static; it adapts to its
            environment just as landscapes shift in response to natural forces and
            cultural change. This project examines adaptation across three temporal
            states—present, death, and revival—while juxtaposing the idealized
            “perfection” of Renaissance form with the evolving realities of both
            body and architecture.
        </p>
        <br />
        <p>
            In the present, human life is abundant, and the city mirrors the
            disparities of its population. Wealthier districts expand outward with
            access to diverse ecological systems, while poorer areas compress into
            dense, survival-driven conditions. At death, the human population and
            pollution reach their peak. The world becomes a fractured, war-like
            artificial landscape where nature has been stripped away, and social
            structures collapse into pack mentalities, leaving outsiders to fend for
            themselves. In revival, the environment enters a phase of
            rehabilitation. Organic matter reclaims the artificial, ecosystems
            regenerate, and life begins to flourish once more.
        </p>
        <br />
        <p>
            Through these three states, the project reflects on how bodies, cities,
            and architectural ideals transform under pressure—revealing that
            perfection is not a fixed condition, but a cycle of decline, adaptation,
            and renewal.
        </p>`
    coverImage={frontmatter.coverImage}
    coverImageAlt={frontmatter.coverImageAlt}
    course="Architectural Studio VI"
    courseDescription="Radicle Roots"
    instructor="Christianna Bennett"
    collaborators="Marinie Ben"
    date="Fall 2022"
    location="Rome, Italy"
>
    <p></p>
</Project>
